#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518} :activity/id #uuid "129cb966-e09b-519a-a368-62dfaa636adb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7781bfa2-360a-52f2-b8a7-5fd12172c6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455124} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad5e1c3-fe87-52c4-9343-a5e560de27a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455128} {:answer/id #uuid "9a55b0b7-ffd1-54c4-8119-7e1a1a488949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455185} {:answer/id #uuid "b6715a23-2351-5f62-8ba5-ef943e459d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} {:answer/id #uuid "c19b819d-c541-51b6-a6e0-7dae5f0106b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd93af99-5bab-55c2-8a85-e0d5efec7517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d78a254d-adb2-5551-a797-0a608524ca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125669" :question/problem "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "23ee9455-766f-5586-95a2-a9ed8dfe87ce"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7d45a6-b7b5-5b6e-a82c-a1748d83e603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454946} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4f36c3-2d0c-50c9-a36d-272fd914e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455133} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f1bdf-26e0-5acd-bbc9-1a8e6be78f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455239} {:answer/id #uuid "c3275529-012a-5a31-82a4-2f28fcd1acb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6f64d6-184a-5394-8a28-a8c004572db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf611c1-7a64-56ff-a8ce-599f171848cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "442b3fed-648b-5cc8-977b-6700ae26738b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125482" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40211c38-7902-5a30-be18-54ba1e524e35" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40242699-9a76-5769-ad29-253e8f600b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455010} {:answer/id #uuid "63642342-0ea8-5389-ae31-2bce908c8ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455082} {:answer/id #uuid "90199dc9-7e02-5cd1-bdd7-df4867251c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455163} {:answer/id #uuid "97eeaf90-7d49-5a72-b321-ee76bbb6e4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} {:answer/id #uuid "c097de0b-9eac-5b4e-97f4-50838319facf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2afbdbe-c2b8-5f8b-8cd1-c52860a3b3d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "afd2a4b0-18e7-5bc4-9563-64bdaef16530" :question/math-academy-id "q-125666" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "48918b6d-e2d1-57bd-906d-d8cb6cd2a9dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029ef420-9eaa-5c6d-8287-3ef33710e976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454907} {:a